 false, "first_name": "efe", "last_name": "rer", "email": "bbbb@gmail.com", "is_active": true, "is_staff": false, "groups": [], "user_permissions": []}}, {"model": "users.useraccount", "pk": 13, "fields": {"password": "pbkdf2_sha256$720000$VyzA9r7DG4qWpN74b0P5ct$gcxchBWpcjTwEGuBD/c/aWBPSV9ikUUP+pd+HThAnmM=", "last_login": null, "is_superuser": false, "first_name": "wewew", "last_name": "wee", "email": "ffffff@gmail.com", "is_active": true, "is_staff": false, "groups": [], "user_permissions": []}}, {"model": "blog.blog", "pk": 1, "fields": {"title": "test1", "subtitle": "test1", "content": "test1", "image": "images/blog/doc_g34e1xy.png"}}, {"model": "blog.blog", "pk": 2, "fields": {"title": "test", "subtitle": "test", "content": "test", "image": "images/blog/doc_eiHlKtb.png"}}, {"model": "blogs.tag", "pk": 1, "fields": {"title": "Architecture"}}, {"model": "blogs.tag", "pk": 2, "fields": {"title": "Planning"}}, {"model": "blogs.tag", "pk": 3, "fields": {"title": "BIM"}}, {"model": "blogs.blog", "pk": 1, "fields": {"title": "Art in Architecture", "image": "images/blogs/art.png", "category": "Art", "author": "Modelflick", "author_pic": "images/blogs/photo_Gt6H8QZ.jpeg", "published_date": "2024-01-28", "reading_time": "5 minutes", "content": "Art in architecture refers to the integration of artistic elements, aesthetics, and design principles into the built environment. It goes beyond the purely functional aspects of buildings and seeks to enhance the visual and sensory experience of the spaces we inhabit. The intersection of art and architecture has been a fundamental aspect of human creativity throughout history, contributing to the cultural identity of societies and reflecting the values and aspirations of different eras.", "tags": "ART"}}, {"model": "blogs.blog", "pk": 2, "fields": {"title": "Building Information modelling", "image": "images/blogs/bim.png", "category": "BIM", "author": "modelflick", "author_pic": "images/blogs/photo.jpeg", "published_date": "2024-01-28", "reading_time": "5 minutes", "content": "Building Information Modeling (BIM) is a collaborative and technology-driven process that involves the generation and management of digital representations of physical and functional characteristics of buildings or infrastructure. It goes beyond traditional 2D drawings and includes three-dimensional (3D) models with intelligent data, providing a comprehensive and integrated approach to the entire life cycle of a construction project.", "tags": "BIM"}}, {"model": "blogs.blog", "pk": 3, "fields": {"title": "Understanding Levels of Development (LOD) in Building Information Modeling (BIM)", "image": "images/blogs/lod.png", "category": "BIM", "author": "Modelflick", "author_pic": "images/blogs/doc.png", "published_date": "2024-09-07", "reading_time": "10 mins", "content": "Building Information Modeling (BIM) has transformed how architectural, engineering, and construction (AEC) professionals design, build, and manage buildings. A key concept within BIM is the Levels of Development (LOD). LOD defines the degree to which a BIM model's elements are developed and detailed at various stages of a project. This framework helps in clarifying the extent of information available and ensures that all stakeholders have a common understanding of what to expect at different project phases.\r\n\r\nWhat is Level of Development (LOD)?\r\nLevel of Development (LOD) refers to the level of detail and reliability of a BIM model's components. Unlike Levels of Detail, which focus on the visual and geometrical aspects of the model, LOD emphasizes the completeness, accuracy, and reliability of the information contained within the model. It provides a standardized way to describe the development of model elements and their usefulness for specific project tasks.\r\n\r\nLevels of Development: An Overview\r\n1. LOD 100 - Conceptual\r\nAt LOD 100, the model is in its conceptual phase. Elements are represented with minimal detail, primarily to convey the general scope and size of the project. The information is often qualitative rather than quantitative, serving as a high-level visual tool.\r\n\r\nCharacteristics: Basic shapes, general locations, and dimensions. The model may include placeholders or general symbols without precise data.\r\n\r\nUsage: Used for early-stage feasibility studies, high-level presentations, and initial design discussions.\r\n\r\n2. LOD 200 - Approximate Geometry\r\nLOD 200 involves an increase in detail compared to LOD 100. Elements are represented with approximate dimensions and locations, offering a clearer understanding of the design but still lacking precise accuracy.\r\n\r\nCharacteristics: Approximate sizes, shapes, and locations of elements. Elements are represented with more detail than LOD 100, though not yet to scale.\r\n\r\nUsage: Suitable for schematic design and coordination purposes. It helps in visualizing the overall design and checking for major conflicts.\r\n\r\n3. LOD 300 - Detailed\r\nAt LOD 300, the model is detailed and accurate. Elements are represented with precise dimensions, materials, and locations, providing a reliable basis for further development and construction.\r\n\r\nCharacteristics: Detailed geometry with accurate dimensions, materials, and component information. The model supports detailed design work and analysis.\r\n\r\nUsage: Ideal for detailed design development, construction documentation, and accurate cost estimation.\r\n\r\n4. LOD 400 - Fabrication\r\nLOD 400 focuses on the level of detail needed for construction and fabrication. The model includes information necessary for the actual construction and assembly of components.\r\n\r\nCharacteristics: Detailed assembly information, including precise component locations and installation methods. The model provides detailed data for fabrication and construction.\r\n\r\nUsage: Used for fabrication, assembly, and construction processes. It ensures that all construction teams have the detailed information needed for accurate execution.\r\n\r\n5. LOD 500 - As-Built\r\nLOD 500 represents the as-built condition of the project. It reflects the actual state of the constructed building, including any changes made during the construction process.\r\n\r\nCharacteristics: Accurate representation of the completed project, including modifications and changes. The model is updated to reflect the actual as-built conditions.\r\n\r\nUsage: Essential for facility management, operations, and maintenance. Provides a reliable reference for ongoing building management and future renovations.\r\n\r\nImportance of LOD in BIM\r\nClear Communication: LOD provides a common language for stakeholders to discuss the level of detail and accuracy of the model, reducing misunderstandings and aligning expectations.\r\n\r\nImproved Coordination: By defining the development level of each component, LOD facilitates better coordination among different teams and disciplines, ensuring that everyone works with the appropriate level of detail.\r\n\r\nEnhanced Accuracy: Accurate and detailed models at higher LODs reduce the likelihood of errors and rework, leading to more reliable project outcomes.\r\n\r\nEfficient Project Management: LOD helps in managing project phases effectively by providing clear milestones and deliverables, ensuring that models meet the required level of development for each stage.\r\n\r\nLifecycle Benefits: An accurate LOD model supports not only the design and construction phases but also the operational and maintenance phases of the building lifecycle, providing valuable information for facility management.", "tags": "BIM"}}, {"model": "projects.project", "pk": 3, "fields": {"client_name": "Renuchandran", "location": "Uliyakovil", "project_type": "Interior", "builtup_area": "3746.00", "project_stage": "Stage4", "start_date": "2024-09-15", "end_date": "2024-09-15", "description": "Test", "image": "images/projects/room.jpg", "passcode": 2222}}, {"model": "projects.projectimage", "pk": 3, "fields": {"project": 3, "image": "images/projects/360degree/room_zQjmD2l.jpg", "image_name": "Bedroom"}}]